nction has a critical point (a corner) at $x=1.$ In this case,  ${f}^{′}(-1)$ does not exist.\n\n- The statement “$f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=3$” is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=3$ since $f(3)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, select only “$f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$.”" 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186329838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329407 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186329838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730202 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186329838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730203 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186329838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730204 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186329838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730205 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186329838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730206 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186329838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730207 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186329838 1071 "![](images/bb/bb8959927fc561f9fc6e021b690c43f4ab57b169aa1526390e02c8d48386bfe3.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the critical points?" 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186329838 1071 "![](images/bb/bb8959927fc561f9fc6e021b690c43f4ab57b169aa1526390e02c8d48386bfe3.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the critical points?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186341964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341581 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186341964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730215 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186341964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730216 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186341964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730217 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186341964 1071 "![](images/43/432991e403167bd560517072d24058c3f2106f093cdcad08f3b5f0ed486f9504.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the critical points?" 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186341964 1071 "![](images/43/432991e403167bd560517072d24058c3f2106f093cdcad08f3b5f0ed486f9504.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the critical points?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186341965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341671 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186341965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730222 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186341965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730223 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186341965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730224 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186341965 1071 "![](images/48/48569328379c72215884b759687c7c40ced06f9864d05d2638e4389694f90630.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points?" 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186341965 1071 "![](images/48/48569328379c72215884b759687c7c40ced06f9864d05d2638e4389694f90630.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186600826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186600741 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186600826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730218 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186600826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730219 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186600826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730220 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186600826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730221 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186600826 1071 "![](images/99/991ced811f1c8f1e337025b6e2f7273d2b028a3af4a376efb8436956dc40fa62.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the critical points?" 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186600826 1071 "![](images/99/991ced811f1c8f1e337025b6e2f7273d2b028a3af4a376efb8436956dc40fa62.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the critical points?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186600837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186600778 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186600837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730212 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186600837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730213 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186600837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730214 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186600837 1071 "![](images/36/36477ec421ff4b7f349a060df762eadee83b008fbfc9e021a8893cc45e405cae.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nI. $f(x)$ has  a stationary point at $x=2$\n\nII. $f(x)$ has  a local maximum at $x=-2$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-2$" 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186600837 1071 "![](images/36/36477ec421ff4b7f349a060df762eadee83b008fbfc9e021a8893cc45e405cae.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186600837 1074 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=2$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=-2$ since $f(-2)\\ge f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough  to $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the graph of the function has a sharp edge at $x=-2$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)$ does not exist.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186600837 1074 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- The statement “$f(x)$ has  a stationary point at $x=2$” is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=2$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- The statement “$f(x)$ has  a local maximum at $x=-2$” is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=-2$ since $f(-2)\\ge f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough  to $x=-2.$\n\n- The statement “$f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-2$” is false. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the graph of the function has a sharp edge at $x=-2$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)$ does not exist.\n\nTherefore, select “$f(x)$ has  a stationary point at $x=2$” and “$f(x)$ has  a local maximum at $x=-2$.”" 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186620891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186620807 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186620891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730198 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186620891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730199 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186620891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730200 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186620891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186730201 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186620891 1071 "![](images/da/daba5c9c96666b81c5e0e3c0623aa97e234b208dc90f9a3f57785bfcc17552d9.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. Given that $f(x)$ is defined on the interval $x\\in [-10,2]$, which points represent the global minimum of $f(x)?$" 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186620891 1071 "![](images/da/daba5c9c96666b81c5e0e3c0623aa97e234b208dc90f9a3f57785bfcc17552d9.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. Given that $f(x)$ is defined on the interval $x\\in [-10,2]$, which points represent the global minimum of $f(x)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186729422 1146 #edb/ref 17592186340002 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186729422 1146 #edb/ref 17592186730186 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186729422 1146 #edb/ref 17592186730187 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186729422 1146 #edb/ref 17592186730189 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186729440 1146 #edb/ref 17592186340531 13194139543303 17592186576411 false] [17592186729440 1146 #edb/ref 17592186730171 13194139543303 17592186576411 true] [17592186